---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { opsYears as opsYearsNow } from '../lib/career.js';
import { versioned } from '../data/asset-version';
import Breadth from '../components/sections/Breadth.astro';
import Cta from '../components/sections/Cta.astro';
import SocialIcon from '../components/SocialIcon.astro';
import { socialLinks, cardEmail } from '../data/links';
import { Mail, QrCode } from '@lucide/astro';

/* REWRITTEN 2026-08-24 under D33. This page used to open on the manufacturer and
   stay there, which made the whole site read as an industrial pitch — the
   operator's own diagnosis of why it was not selling.

   The career is now the pitch, and the facts and their sourcing live in
   docs/career-record.md. Two names on this page are deliberate operator
   decisions, not defaults:

     • Jimmy Buffett's Margaritaville at Mohegan Sun is NAMED. It is his own
       employment history, published on resumes sent externally, and it is the
       detail that proves the scale — a casino restaurant turns more covers in a
       night than most New Bern rooms do in a week.

     • The psychiatric center is NOT named. "A clinical practice" carries the
       entire credential — patient scheduling and records under federal privacy
       law — without putting a mental-health facility's name on a sales page.
       Decided 2026-08-24; do not "improve" it by adding the name back.

   THE APPLIANCE PLANT IS STILL NEVER NAMED (D1, D8). Every local resolves "the
   appliance plant in town" instantly, and it borrows nobody's branding.

   NO PERCENTAGES. Nine of them exist across the resumes and every one is held,
   because no underlying calculation exists for any of them and a number a client
   can ask about is a number that needs working.

   ── THREE CHANGES, 2026-09-13, ALL OPERATOR CALLS ──────────────────────────

   1. THIS PAGE NO LONGER PRINTS A COUNT. It said "five trades" three times, in
      the meta description, in the run's closing line, and through Breadth's
      capability row. The home page's opening band said "Six domains that share
      nothing" — and the navigation on EVERY page, /bio/'s own note in
      src/data/site-map.js, said "Five trades before the software". So a reader
      met both numbers without leaving the home page.

      Underneath it the two lists were counting different things. The beats in
      src/lib/trades.js are DOMAINS drawn from four employers; this page counts
      EMPLOYERS. The licensed insurance practice below was in this list, in
      docs/career-record.md, and in no beat of the line a visitor reads first.
      Insurance was appended to that array the same day. One list counts itself
      now; nothing else counts, and scripts/check-dist.mjs refuses a build where
      a rendered page prints a second count.

   2. THE H1 IS HIS NAME. It was "Before I built software, I ran the businesses."
      — the home page's h1, word for word, which src/data/home.ts:160 recorded as
      a duplicate that "has to break on the OTHER page". He chose those words for
      the home page, so this is the page that moves. The claim they made has not
      been thrown away: it is the sentence the whole run below argues.

   3. THERE IS A PHOTOGRAPH. This page existed to prove a person is behind the
      practice and carried no img tag at all. scripts/gen-portrait.mjs cuts it,
      and refuses to write one with the second person still in frame.

   THE BAND SHAPES ARE D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE. Every band varies at
   least two of the heavy signals — ground, width, density, type pairing —
   because the complaint that started D113 was that every section looked like
   the one before it. Hero is feature-width and two columns; the run is a narrow
   popout on a surface ground with tall rhythm; Breadth is the dark instrument
   cut; Connect is a deep ground at tight rhythm. The `/ 01` and `/ 03` numerals
   are gone: PICKUP scores decorative section numerals as a generated-design
   signal, and these are not steps in a sequence. */

/* Derived, never a literal — the same rule as the tool count and for the same
   reason: a hardcoded number is wrong every January and nothing would error.
   IMPORTED, not re-declared. This page carried its own copy of the start date
   and its own division for it, which is two places for one fact and no way to
   notice when they stop agreeing. src/lib/career.js is the one copy, and the
   share-card generator already reads the same module. */
const opsYears = opsYearsNow();
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="Ian Provencher"
  description={`Ian Provencher — ${opsYears} years running operations across a casino restaurant floor, banking, a clinical practice, insurance and manufacturing. Now building the software every one of them needed.`}
  image="/og/bio.png"
>
  {/* ── Hero: name, the run, and the standing portrait ─────────────────────
      FEATURE WIDTH AND TWO COLUMNS. The home page's opener is the only other
      band on the site at this width, and it puts a screenshot in the second
      column. This one puts a person there, which is the whole difference
      between the two pages' arguments. */}
  {/* DENSITY IS THE DEFAULT HERE ON PURPOSE. `tall` was tried first and the band
      paid for its height twice — the portrait is 530px on its own and already
      sets the grid row, so the extra padding left a third of the band empty
      under the text. The variation this band carries is feature width, two
      columns and the grid backdrop; it does not need a fourth. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>

    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr_0.8fr] lg:items-center lg:gap-16">
      <div>
        <p class="mono-label">Bio</p>
        <h1 class="statement mt-3">Ian Provencher</h1>
        <p class="statement-sub mt-5 max-w-2xl">
          A restaurant floor at a casino, a bank’s lending division, a clinic’s front office, an insurance book,
          and now the planning floor of the appliance plant in town.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          {opsYears} years of running operations before a line of code. Not advising any of them.
          Running them.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          Every one of them ran on something that almost worked, and none of them was ever going to
          get software built for it. That’s the whole reason AppliedIQ exists.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* The photograph is a real capture (D26, D113) — Oct 2021, cropped by
          scripts/gen-portrait.mjs, which re-scans its own output for the second
          person's dress and refuses to write a file that still contains her. */}
      {/* Centered while the columns are stacked, hard right once they are not.
          `.portrait-tall` caps its own width, so left-aligning it on a phone
          left it hanging off the text's measure rather than sitting with it. */}
      <div class="flex justify-center lg:block lg:justify-self-end">
        <img
          class="portrait-tall"
          src={versioned('/ian-provencher-full.webp')}
          alt="Ian Provencher, standing outdoors in a navy waistcoat and tie"
          width="520"
          height="720"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The run — a narrow column on a surface ground, tall rhythm ────────── */}
  <Section tone="surface" width="popout" density="tall">
    <p class="mono-label">The run</p>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        I started on a restaurant floor at Jimmy Buffett’s Margaritaville inside Mohegan Sun, and
        stayed five and a half years — supervisor first, then operations manager and head of the
        beverage department. That meant the schedule, the staffing, the inventory, the pour cost, and
        a profit-and-loss statement I had to explain every month. A casino restaurant turns more
        covers in a night than most rooms do in a week, and none of it was run on software built for
        it. It was run on spreadsheets and on me.
      </p>
      <p>
        From there, banking. I ran consumer lending operations at United Bank in Hartford — the
        day-to-day, the policies, the hiring and training — then cash management at Dime Bank, where
        the work was financial reporting and closing the books clean enough to survive an audit.
        Different building, same shape of problem: the system covered most of it, and a person
        covered the rest by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then a clinical practice, where I ran patient scheduling and treatment records under federal
        privacy rules. If you run a dental office or a family practice, you already know that work.
        After that a licensed insurance practice — reading policy documents to people who needed them
        explained, and working out what someone actually needed rather than what was easiest to sell.
      </p>
      <p>
        And now supply chain at the appliance plant in town. Materials planning, bills of
        material, supplier performance, and the ordering logic that decides whether a production line
        runs on Monday. If a part is missing, the line stops — so the whole discipline is knowing what
        you are about to run out of, long before you run out of it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink">
        One problem, every single time: <span class="aiq-accent">the software almost fit</span>, and
        somebody was quietly holding the rest together.
      </p>
      <p>
        That gap used to be permanent, because the software that would have closed it was priced for
        companies a hundred times the size. That stopped being true. I build those tools now, and the
        business that gets one owns it outright — codebase, data, accounts in its own name.
      </p>
      <p>
        The AI half isn’t new either. It ran alongside the operations work the whole way, and it’s now
        formally part of the work — shaping the path, scope and implementations of AI across the
        company’s North America region. But it’s the leverage, not the point. The point is that a shop
        running on a spreadsheet can now have a real tool built around exactly how it works — no
        enterprise budget, no license, no asking permission.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wasn’t born here. I chose it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base">
        See where that’s pointed —{' '}
        <a href="/main-street/" class="link-sweep">
          my focus on local small business →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── Beyond the moat — the dark instrument cut ───────────────────────────
      The hard cut between a warm page and a dark chassis is the heaviest signal
      available, and this is the band where the proof-at-scale argument lives.
      index="" drops the numeral; see the note at the top of this file. */}
  <Breadth tone="instrument" index="" />

  {/* ── Connect — deep ground, tight rhythm ────────────────────────────────
      A short band of buttons does not want the standard vertical rhythm, and
      the deep ground closes the page off from the dark band above it without
      going back to plain canvas. */}
  <Section tone="deep" density="tight">
    <p class="mono-label">Connect</p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      The direct lines — no funnel, no gatekeeper.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      {socialLinks.map((s) => (
        <a href={s.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-secondary">
          <SocialIcon path={s.iconPath} size={16} />
          {s.name}
        </a>
      ))}
      <a href={`mailto:${cardEmail}`} class="btn btn-secondary">
        <Mail size={16} aria-hidden="true" />
        Email
      </a>
      <a href="/card/" class="btn btn-secondary">
        <QrCode size={16} aria-hidden="true" />
        Digital card
      </a>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── Closing CTA ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <Cta />
</MarketingLayout>
